---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
const wives = [
  ["Magdelena Elisabeth De Beer", "1754–1843", "Huguenot-descended Cape", "m. Gerthardus Lodewikus Booysen. Lived to 89. Her husband has no dates at all — she is better recorded than he is."],
  ["Anna Maria van der Merwe", "1785 – bef. Mar 1825", "one of the largest Cape families", "m. 2 Nov 1805, Graaff-Reinet. Her husband was about seventeen."],
  ["Johanna Catharina Mountjoy", "1819–1863", "English", "m. 4 Nov 1838, Cradock. Died at Zevenfontein aged 44. Gives “Mountjoy” to the family."],
  ["Catherine Maria Sophia “Kathleen” Barry", "1859–1940", "Irish / English at the Cape", "m. 22 Dec 1879 in the DRC at Barkly, after banns. Thirteen children. Occupation: Needle Woman. Died at Mayfair, Johannesburg."],
  ["Anna Johanna Maria Pretorius", "1906–1944", "Afrikaner — and a Barry granddaughter", "m. 30 Dec 1924, Vereeniging. Six children. Died at 37 of a ruptured extra-uterine pregnancy. Born 1906, not 1905 as the tree has it."],
  ["Gertruida Alexandra Kinnear / Kinnier", "—", "Scottish", "second wife of George Downing Mountjoy Booyzen. Two daughters, both named Gertrude Elizabeth, both died as infants."],
];
const jobs = [
  ["Margaretha Wilhelmina Elizabeth Mary Kolbe", "1826–1912", "LAUNDRESS"],
  ["Catherine Maria Sophia “Kathleen” Barry", "1859–1940", "Needle Woman — and, at her death, “Home Duties” and “Military Pensioner”. No son of hers is among the First World War dead; the pension must come from one who served and survived."],
  ["Margarietha Wilhelmina Elizabeth Barry", "1880–1961", "Housewife"],
];
---
<Base title="The women"
      eyebrow="Mountjoy · Barry · Kolbe · Downing · Pretorius · Schleher · van der Merwe · De Beer"
      dek="The male line of this family is Cape Dutch and almost unbroken. Everything else about it — its forenames, its politics, its religion, its 1820 Settler descent and its German origins — arrived through the women. This page puts them back at the front.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The wives of the direct line</h2>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:17rem">Name</th><th>Dates</th><th>Family</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{wives.map(([n, d, f, x]) => (
        <tr><td><strong>{n}</strong></td><td class="m">{d}</td><td>{f}</td><td class="cap">{x}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:22px">
    A Cape Dutch male line that married <strong>British women in three successive generations</strong> —
    Mountjoy in 1838, Barry in 1879, Kinnear in the 1940s — is not a coincidence of the district. It is the
    whole character of the family.
  </blockquote>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>They are inside the men's names</h2>
  <p class="dek">David Defranceski's great-grandfather was christened <b>George Downing Mountjoy
    Booyzen</b>. On an Afrikaans surname that reads like an affectation. It is the opposite: every one of
    those forenames is a woman's surname.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:9rem">Name</th><th>Comes from</th><th>Who</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>George</strong></td><td><strong>George Augustus Kolbe</strong> (1802–1844)</td>
          <td>his great-great-grandfather — the 1820 Settler</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Downing</strong></td><td><strong>Margaret Georgina Elizabeth Downing</strong> (1803–1867)</td>
          <td>George Kolbe's wife; married at Shoreditch aged about fifteen</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Mountjoy</strong></td><td><strong>Johanna Catharina Mountjoy</strong> (1819–1863)</td>
          <td>his great-grandmother, married at Cradock in 1838</td></tr>
        <tr><td><em>Booyzen</em></td><td><strong>Willem Hermanus Booyzen</strong> (1851–1905)</td>
          <td>his father — who changed it from <em>Booysen</em></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px">The pattern repeats across the family: <b>David Kolbe Booyzen</b> (1896–1980)
    carries the Kolbe surname outright; <b>John Barry Booyzen</b> (1884–1952) carries Barry;
    <b>Augustas Kolbe Enslin</b> (1853–1937) carries Kolbe into a third family. And
    <b>Frederik Fortsenatus Booÿsen</b> (b. 1899) is a worn-down copy of <b>Frederik Fortunatus Kolbe</b>
    (b. 1839), by way of <b>Frederick Fortunatus Barry</b> (b. 1863).</p>
  <blockquote>The women's families did not vanish into the Booyzen name. <strong>They were carried inside
    it.</strong></blockquote>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>They worked</h2>
  <p class="dek">Three occupations are recorded for women in this ancestry. All three are manual.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:20rem">Name</th><th>Dates</th><th>Occupation as recorded</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{jobs.map(([n, d, o]) => (
        <tr><td>{n}</td><td class="m">{d}</td><td><strong>{o}</strong></td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">Set beside the men — <b>carpenter</b>, <b>builder</b>, <b>grocer</b>,
    <b>labourer</b>, <b>tailor</b>, <b>chemist</b> — this is not a gentry family at any point in its
    recorded history, on either side of the marriage. The <b>Barrys of Swellendam</b>, the great Cape
    merchant house, are a different Barry family unless and until a document says otherwise, and this
    archive makes no claim on them.</p>
</section>

<Rule name="gable" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>The dead children are named after each other</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>Catherina Maria Sophia Barry</b> died at Burgersdorp on <b>30 July 1859</b>, aged four. Her sister
      <b>Catherine Maria Sophia Barry</b> was born on <b>4 August 1859 — five days later</b>, and was given
      her name.</p>
    <p>That name then travelled: <b>Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen (1891–1946)</b>, and
      <b>Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen (1929–2013)</b> — David Defranceski's grandmother, who died on
      Christmas Day 2013 in Johannesburg still carrying the name of a four-year-old who died in a Karoo
      village in 1859.</p>
    <p>It happens again, and harder. After Anna Pretorius died in 1944, George remarried and had a daughter
      <b>Gertrude Elizabeth</b>, born 11 May 1946, dead four days later at <b>85 Rheeden Street, Forest
      Hill</b>. A second <b>Gertrude Elizabeth</b> is recorded born <b>11 May 1947</b> — the same day of the
      year — and dead within it. <span class="chip inf">Unverified</span> The exact date repetition may
      mean a duplicated record rather than a second child; it needs checking either way.</p>
    <p>And <b>Anna Johanna Maria Pretorius</b> was named after her own grandmother,
      <b>Anna Johanna Maria Schoemann</b> — the tree records the intention explicitly, and the 1923
      <em>Sterfkennis</em> of that grandmother names <em>her</em> parents in turn as
      <b>Ruben Schoeman</b> and <b>Magdalena Johanna Botha</b> of Barkly Oost.
      <a href={u("/documents/")}>The documents &rarr;</a></p>
    <p class="cap"><b>A correction.</b> This archive previously wrote that Anna was born on 3 October 1905,
      "five weeks after her infant brother Hendrik Nikolaas died." Her baptism register gives her birth as
      <b>3 October 1906</b>, and her death form gives her age as <b>37</b>, which agrees. The gap was
      <b>thirteen months</b>, not five weeks, and <b>the sentence is withdrawn</b>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Anna Johanna Maria Pretorius, 1906–1944</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> Born at <b>Barkly East</b> on <b>3 October 1906</b> —
      not 1905, as the tree has it. Three records agree against it: the baptism register (<em>geb. 3 Oct.
      1906</em>), her 1944 death form (<em>age 37</em>) and her marriage register (<em>age 18</em>, with
      her father's written consent). Married at <b>Vereeniging</b> on 30 December 1924, <b>aged
      eighteen</b>. Six children between 1925 and 1934.
      <a href={u("/documents/")}>The one-digit slip &rarr;</a></p>
    <p>She died on <b>26 February 1944 at 85 Rheeders Street, Forest Hill, Johannesburg</b>, aged
      <b>37</b>. Her death form gives the cause in full: <b>"Haemorrhage due to Ruptured Tubal Pregnancy
      (extra-uterine). Not 1st child."</b> An ectopic pregnancy, which in 1944 in a Johannesburg suburb
      was very often fatal. She is buried at <b>West Park Cemetery</b> — the same ground as her husband.</p>
    <p>She left six children, the eldest eighteen and the youngest ten. <b>Catherine was fourteen.</b> Her
      husband remarried, lost two infant daughters, and died himself of a heart attack on 21 February 1947.
      <b>Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen was eighteen years old and had no parents.</b> Two years later she
      married Nuno Fernando Lerena at St Joseph's, Mayfair.
      <a href={u("/johannesburg/")}>Johannesburg &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Margaret Downing signed with a cross</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The <b>1819 marriage register</b> at St Leonard's Shoreditch records the entry in the usual form —
      and then, where the bride should sign:</p>
    <blockquote><b>Margaret Downing † her mark.</b></blockquote>
    <p><b>She could not write her own name.</b> She was fifteen years old, and had that morning been
      sworn to be twenty-one and upwards. Her husband signed — after striking through a first attempt.</p>
    <p>Forty-eight years later she died at <b>Burgersdorp</b> owning <b>a dwelling house and a farm in
      the division of Albert</b>, having buried her husband at forty-one and raised ten children on a
      mission circuit from Uitenhage to Bethulie and Philippolis.</p>
    <blockquote>It is the steepest arc in this archive, and it begins with a cross in a Shoreditch
      register. <a href={u("/documents/")}>The entry &rarr;</a></blockquote>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="thorn" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>Her brother's family was still there in 1889</h2>
  <p class="dek">Reading a Booyzen baptism in the 1889 register of Barkly Oost, the archive noticed
    <strong>Mountjoys on the facing page</strong>. They turned out to be family.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:20px">
    <pre class="mono" style="line-height:1.5;white-space:pre;margin:0;padding:20px;background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:3px">{`JAMES MOUNTJOY (1786-1827)  ===  ANNA CATHARINA SLIER / SCHLEHER (1787- )
                m. 9 March 1817, GRAAFF-REINET
                          |
     +--------------------+---------------------+
     |                                          |
JOHANNA CATHARINA MOUNTJOY            GEORGE MOUNTJOY (b. 1823, Cradock)
  (1819-1863)                          SADDLE MAKER
  === Petrus Jacobus Booysen            === Maria Sophia Botha (1827-1911)
      m. 1838, Cradock                      m. 20 Aug 1848, CRADOCK
     |                                          |   (13 children)
  THE BOOYZEN LINE            +-----------------+-----------------+
                              |                                   |
                  JAMES JONATHAN MOUNTJOY          HERCULES JOHANNES MOUNTJOY
                   (1854 Burgersdorp -              (c.1866 - 1941 Wodehouse)
                    1937 BARKLY EAST)                === Maria Sophia van Niekerk
                   = the 1889 witness                    m. 8 Oct 1888, DORDRECHT
                                                        |
                                             GEORGE MOUNTJOY (1889-1963)
                                             = the child baptised at
                                               Barkly Oost, 6 Oct 1889`}</pre>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:24px">
    <p><b>George Mountjoy, saddle maker of Cradock, was Johanna Catharina's younger brother.</b> The two
      Mountjoy men on that register page — <b>Hercules Johannes</b> as father and <b>Jakobus Jonathan</b>
      as witness — are his sons.</p>
    <p>It fits to the month: Hercules Johannes married at <b>Dordrecht on 8 October 1888</b>; his first
      child <b>George was born 21 July 1889</b> and baptised at Barkly Oost that October.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Booyzen</th><th>Mountjoy</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:9rem">Child</td><td><strong>Johanna Catharina Booyzen</strong>, b. 20 June 1889</td><td><strong>George Mountjoy</strong>, b. 21 July 1889</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Baptised</td><td><strong>15 September 1889</strong>, Barkly Oost</td><td><strong>6 October 1889</strong>, Barkly Oost</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Father</td><td>Willem Hermanus Booysen</td><td>Hercules Johannes Mountjoy</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Godparents</td><td><strong>John Barry and Margaret W. E. Kolby</strong> — her grandparents</td><td>van Niekerk and Mountjoy kin</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:24px">
    The two children were <strong>second cousins, born a month apart and baptised three weeks apart in the
    same church.</strong> Their fathers were first cousins. And the Booyzen daughter was christened
    <strong>Johanna Catharina</strong> — after the great-aunt who is the link between them.
  </blockquote>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>And the Mountjoys stayed.</b> James Jonathan died at <b>Barkly East</b> in 1937; Hercules Johannes
    in <b>Wodehouse</b> in 1941; Maria Sophia van Niekerk lived until <b>1961</b>. While the Booyzens took
    the train to Johannesburg, their Mountjoy cousins remained in the district for another half-century.
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:20px"><b>Sources.</b> The 1889 register read by this archive; the
    1820 Settlers database (James Jonathan <span class="m">I145380</span>); the FamilySearch Family Tree
    (George Mountjoy <span class="m">GVQ6-TDR</span>, 31 sources; Hercules Johannes
    <span class="m">MG9W-8F6</span>, 23); and <em>South Africa, Civil Marriage Records</em>, George
    Mountjoy and Maria Sophia Botha, Cradock, 20 Aug 1848, certificate <b>735</b>.
    The 1823 baptism, the 1848 marriage and the 1888 marriage are record-backed.</p>
  <div class="note">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>This no longer depends on a user-edited tree.</strong> The caution that
      used to stand here — that the <em>arrangement</em> of the family was FamilySearch's — can be
      withdrawn. The <b>Dutch Reformed baptism register</b> records <b>Herculas Johannes Mountjoy, born
      16 July 1866, baptised 29 July 1866, father George Mountjoy, mother Maria Sophia Botha</b>. His
      1941 death registration gives his age as <b>74</b>, which fits a July 1866 birth exactly, and his
      marriage at <b>Dordrecht on 8 October 1888, aged 22</b>, falls a year before the 1889 font. George
      Mountjoy → Hercules Johannes is now a church register.
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>The full search &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:16px"><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> And Johanna Catharina Mountjoy
    herself now has a <b>birth date</b>. Her 1863 death notice gives her age as
    <b>"44 years &amp; 19 days"</b> — the form asked only for years. Counted back from 19 May 1863 that
    is <strong>30 April 1819</strong>, at <b>Graaff Reinet</b>, which the same document gives as her
    birthplace. <a href={u("/documents/")}>The death notice &rarr;</a></p>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px"><b>Four corrections it offers our own tree:</b> Anna Catharina is
    recorded there as <b>Slier</b>, not Schleher; her marriage to James Mountjoy is dated
    <b>9 March 1817 at Graaff-Reinet</b>; <b>Thomas Jacobus lived to 1827</b>, not 1818; and there is a
    sibling our tree lacks entirely — <b>Jacoba Catharina Mountjoy (1820–1842)</b>.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Johanna Margaritha Pretorius — the woman four documents refused to name</h2>
  <p class="dek">She was the hardest person in this archive to find, and the reason is worth as much as
    the answer.</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Petrus Jacobus Booysen's first wife died in <b>1863</b>. His own death notice of <b>1884</b> enters
      him as <b>married</b> — twenty-one years later — and names a child, <b>Gerrit Jacobus, born 19
      January 1870</b>, who could only be another woman's. Three children in all were born after 1863,
      and the family tree had attached every one of them to a woman already in her grave.</p>
    <p>So the archive knew she existed, and went looking. <b>It looked in the wrong kind of document, for
      months.</b></p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>His 1884 death notice</b> proves her and never asks her name. The form has no line for a
        wife.</li>
      <li><b>Her son Stephanus Francois's death notice of 1940</b> does have a line for his mother. His
        widow wrote <b>"oorlede"</b> — deceased.</li>
      <li>Her <b>own</b> death notice, if one exists, has never been found under any spelling.</li>
    </ul>
    <blockquote>Every route ran through a death, and death is exactly where this family stops
      remembering its women.</blockquote>
    <p><b>She was named by a christening.</b> A baptism register asks for both parents, at the moment
      when everyone concerned is alive and standing in the same room. Gert Jacobus was baptised on
      <b>19 June 1870</b>:</p>
    <p class="m" style="font-size:16px;margin-top:10px"><b>Oud: Petrus Jacobus Booyzen en Johanna
      Margaritha Pretorius</b></p>
    <p style="margin-top:14px">And at the font stood <b>Petrus Jacobus Booijzen junior</b> (b. 1839) and
      <b>Magdalena Johanna Francina Booijzen</b> (b. 1853) — the third and sixth children of the first
      marriage, on the 1863 death notice. Two of the dead wife's children godparented the new wife's
      first baby. <a href={u("/documents/")}>The register &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Three times the form asked, and three times it was the mother who was gone</h2>
  <p class="dek">This is the clearest thing the documents say about women in this family, and it was not
    visible until the third one was read.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m" style="width:7rem">Written</th><th style="width:15rem">Death notice of</th><th>"Names of the Parents of the Deceased"</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">1840</td><td><b>James Montjoy</b></td><td><b>"Not Known."</b> Both parents, together.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1884</td><td><b>Petrus Jacobus Booysen</b></td><td>Father written out in full. <b>"Mother's name, not known"</b> — underlined.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1915</td><td><b>Margaret Elizabeth Barry née Kolbe</b></td><td>"Father: Kolbe, thinks James was christian name. Mother: <b>Unknown</b>."</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p>Read down the right-hand column. In <b>1884</b> the father has a full name and the mother has none.
      In <b>1915</b> the father survives as a surname and a guess; the mother is <em>Unknown</em>. Only in
      <b>1840</b>, the oldest of the three, do both disappear at once.</p>
    <p>The women in this archive are not missing because the records failed. <b>They are missing because
      the men filling in the forms could not remember them</b> — and the archive can name three of them
      anyway, from other documents, because someone eventually looked.</p>
    <p><b>Anna Maria van der Merwe</b> is the 1884 blank. She had died before March 1825, when her son was
      twelve; fifty-nine years later nobody in his house could give her name.
      <a href={u("/documents/")}>The three notices &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The oldest woman in the archive</h2>
  <p><b>Anna Catharina Sleer / Slier / Schleher</b> (1787–1885), mother of Johanna Catharina Mountjoy,
    lived to <b>ninety-eight</b>. <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> Her daughter's
    <b>1863 death notice</b> names her, in the informant's hand, as <b>Anna Catharina Sleer</b> — the
    first time this archive has had her from a document rather than from a tree. The hand is ambiguous
    between <em>Sleer</em> and <em>Steer</em>, and it is settled from outside: the tree carries
    <b>Schleher</b>, FamilySearch <b>Slier</b>. Three renderings, three independent sources, all with an
    <b>l</b> and none with a <b>t</b>.
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> Her first marriage is now on record too: her second husband's
    <b>1840 death notice</b> describes her as <b>"Catharina Sl[i/e]er, widow of William Pietersen"</b>.
    The archive had only inferred the Pieterzen marriage, from a godparent standing at an 1851 font.
    Here it is named, in a document eleven years older.
    She married a <b>Pieterzen</b> first — four sons between 1808 and 1813 — and then
    <b>James Mountjoy</b>, who died in 1827 leaving her with a son, <b>Willem Jan</b>, born and dead in
    1828. She outlived her second husband by <b>fifty-eight years</b>.</p>
  <p class="cap">Schleher is a third German surname in this ancestry, alongside Kolbe of Württemberg and
    the Kohlbeck/Larman circle of German Lutheran Soho — and, if the Booysen progenitor is right, a fourth
    on the male line. <a href={u("/name/")}>The name &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
</Base>
